.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Sucrase"
.PP
\fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP [](LICENSE) \fR\fP
.SH "\fRTry it out\fP"
.PP
.SH "Quick usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
yarn add \-\-dev sucrase  # Or npm install \-\-save\-dev sucrase
node \-r sucrase/register main\&.ts
.fi
.PP

.PP
Using the \fRts-node\fP integration:

.PP
.PP
.nf
yarn add \-\-dev sucrase ts\-node typescript
\&./node_modules/\&.bin/ts\-node \-\-transpiler sucrase/ts\-node\-plugin main\&.ts
.fi
.PP
.SH "Project overview"
.PP
Sucrase is an alternative to Babel that allows super-fast development builds\&. Instead of compiling a large range of JS features to be able to work in Internet Explorer, Sucrase assumes that you're developing with a recent browser or recent Node\&.js version, so it focuses on compiling non-standard language extensions: JSX, TypeScript, and Flow\&. Because of this smaller scope, Sucrase can get away with an architecture that is much more performant but less extensible and maintainable\&. Sucrase's parser is forked from Babel's parser (so Sucrase is indebted to Babel and wouldn't be possible without it) and trims it down to a focused subset of what Babel solves\&. If it fits your use case, hopefully Sucrase can speed up your development experience!

.PP
\fBSucrase has been extensively tested\&.\fP It can successfully build the \fRBenchling\fP frontend code, \fRBabel\fP, \fRReact\fP, \fRTSLint\fP, \fRApollo client\fP, and \fRdecaffeinate\fP with all tests passing, about 1 million lines of code total\&.

.PP
\fBSucrase is about 20x faster than Babel\&.\fP Here's one measurement of how Sucrase compares with other tools when compiling the Jest codebase 3 times, about 360k lines of code total:

.PP
.PP
.nf
            Time            Speed
Sucrase     0\&.57 seconds    636975 lines per second
swc         1\&.19 seconds    304526 lines per second
esbuild     1\&.45 seconds    248692 lines per second
TypeScript  8\&.98 seconds    40240 lines per second
Babel       9\&.18 seconds    39366 lines per second
.fi
.PP

.PP
Details: Measured on July 2022\&. Tools run in single-threaded mode without warm-up\&. See the \fRbenchmark code\fP for methodology and caveats\&.
.SH "Transforms"
.PP
The main configuration option in Sucrase is an array of transform names\&. These transforms are available:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBjsx\fP: Enables JSX syntax\&. By default, JSX is transformed to \fRReact\&.createClass\fP, but may be preserved or transformed to \fR_jsx()\fP by setting the \fRjsxRuntime\fP option\&. Also adds \fRcreateReactClass\fP display names and JSX context information\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBtypescript\fP: Compiles TypeScript code to JavaScript, removing type annotations and handling features like enums\&. Does not check types\&. Sucrase transforms each file independently, so you should enable the \fRisolatedModules\fP TypeScript flag so that the typechecker will disallow the few features like \fRconst enum\fPs that need cross-file compilation\&. The Sucrase option \fRkeepUnusedImports\fP can be used to disable all automatic removal of imports and exports, analogous to TS \fRverbatimModuleSyntax\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBflow\fP: Removes Flow type annotations\&. Does not check types\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBimports\fP: Transforms ES Modules (\fRimport\fP/\fRexport\fP) to CommonJS (\fRrequire\fP/\fRmodule\&.exports\fP) using the same approach as Babel and TypeScript with \fR--esModuleInterop\fP\&. If \fRpreserveDynamicImport\fP is specified in the Sucrase options, then dynamic \fRimport\fP expressions are left alone, which is particularly useful in Node to load ESM-only libraries\&. If \fRpreserveDynamicImport\fP is not specified, \fRimport\fP expressions are transformed into a promise-wrapped call to \fRrequire\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBreact-hot-loader\fP: Performs the equivalent of the \fRreact-hot-loader/babel\fP transform in the \fRreact-hot-loader\fP project\&. This enables advanced hot reloading use cases such as editing of bound methods\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBjest\fP: Hoist desired \fRjest\fP method calls above imports in the same way as \fRbabel-plugin-jest-hoist\fP\&. Does not validate the arguments passed to \fRjest\&.mock\fP, but the same rules still apply\&.
.PP

.PP
When the \fRimports\fP transform is \fInot\fP specified (i\&.e\&. when targeting ESM), the \fRinjectCreateRequireForImportRequire\fP option can be specified to transform TS \fRimport foo = require("foo");\fP in a way that matches the \fRTypeScript 4\&.7 behavior\fP with \fRmodule: nodenext\fP\&.

.PP
These newer JS features are transformed by default:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fROptional chaining\fP: \fRa?\&.b\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRNullish coalescing\fP: \fRa ?? b\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRClass fields\fP: \fRclass C { x = 1; }\fP\&. This includes static fields but not the \fR#x\fP private field syntax\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRNumeric separators\fP: \fRconst n = 1_234;\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fROptional catch binding\fP: \fRtry { doThing(); } catch { }\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
If your target runtime supports these features, you can specify \fRdisableESTransforms: true\fP so that Sucrase preserves the syntax rather than trying to transform it\&. Note that transpiled and standard class fields behave slightly differently; see the \fRTypeScript 3\&.7 release notes\fP for details\&. If you use TypeScript, you can enable the TypeScript option \fRuseDefineForClassFields\fP to enable error checking related to these differences\&.
.SS "Unsupported syntax"
All JS syntax not mentioned above will "pass through" and needs to be supported by your JS runtime\&. For example:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Decorators, private fields, \fRthrow\fP expressions, generator arrow functions, and \fRdo\fP expressions are all unsupported in browsers and Node (as of this writing), and Sucrase doesn't make an attempt to transpile them\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Object rest/spread, async functions, and async iterators are all recent features that should work fine, but might cause issues if you use older versions of tools like webpack\&. BigInt and newer regex features may or may not work, based on your tooling\&.
.PP
.SS "JSX Options"
By default, JSX is compiled to React functions in development mode\&. This can be configured with a few options:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBjsxRuntime\fP: A string specifying the transform mode, which can be one of three values:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR"classic"\fP (default): The original JSX transform that calls \fRReact\&.createElement\fP by default\&. To configure for non-React use cases, specify:
.IP "    \(bu" 6
\fBjsxPragma\fP: Element creation function, defaults to \fRReact\&.createElement\fP\&.
.IP "    \(bu" 6
\fBjsxFragmentPragma\fP: Fragment component, defaults to \fRReact\&.Fragment\fP\&.
.PP

.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR"automatic"\fP: The \fRnew JSX transform\fP introduced with React 17, which calls \fRjsx\fP functions and auto-adds import statements\&. To configure for non-React use cases, specify:
.IP "    \(bu" 6
\fBjsxImportSource\fP: Package name for auto-generated import statements, defaults to \fRreact\fP\&.
.PP

.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR"preserve"\fP: Don't transform JSX, and instead emit it as-is in the output code\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBproduction\fP: If \fRtrue\fP, use production version of functions and don't include debugging information\&. When using React in production mode with the automatic transform, this \fImust\fP be set to true to avoid an error about \fRjsxDEV\fP being missing\&.
.PP
.SS "Legacy CommonJS interop"
Two legacy modes can be used with the \fRimports\fP transform:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBenableLegacyTypeScriptModuleInterop\fP: Use the default TypeScript approach to CommonJS interop instead of assuming that TypeScript's \fR--esModuleInterop\fP flag is enabled\&. For example, if a CJS module exports a function, legacy TypeScript interop requires you to write \fRimport * as add from '\&./add';\fP, while Babel, Webpack, Node\&.js, and TypeScript with \fR--esModuleInterop\fP require you to write \fRimport add from '\&./add';\fP\&. As mentioned in the \fRdocs\fP, the TypeScript team recommends you always use \fR--esModuleInterop\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBenableLegacyBabel5ModuleInterop\fP: Use the Babel 5 approach to CommonJS interop, so that you can run \fRrequire('\&./MyModule')\fP instead of \fRrequire('\&./MyModule')\&.default\fP\&. Analogous to \fRbabel-plugin-add-module-exports\fP\&.
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.SS "Tool integrations"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRWebpack\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGulp\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRJest\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRRollup\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRBroccoli\fP
.PP
.SS "Usage in Node"
The most robust way is to use the Sucrase plugin for \fRts-node\fP, which has various Node integrations and configures Sucrase via \fRtsconfig\&.json\fP: 
.PP
.nf
ts\-node \-\-transpiler sucrase/ts\-node\-plugin

.fi
.PP

.PP
For projects that don't target ESM, Sucrase also has a require hook with some reasonable defaults that can be accessed in a few ways:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
From code: \fRrequire("sucrase/register");\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
When invoking Node: \fRnode -r sucrase/register main\&.ts\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
As a separate binary: \fRsucrase-node main\&.ts\fP
.PP

.PP
Options can be passed to the require hook via a \fRSUCRASE_OPTIONS\fP environment variable holding a JSON string of options\&.
.SS "Compiling a project to JS"
For simple use cases, Sucrase comes with a \fRsucrase\fP CLI that mirrors your directory structure to an output directory: 
.PP
.nf
sucrase \&./srcDir \-d \&./outDir \-\-transforms typescript,imports

.fi
.PP
.SS "Usage from code"
For any advanced use cases, Sucrase can be called from JS directly:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import {transform} from "sucrase";
const compiledCode = transform(code, {transforms: ["typescript", "imports"]})\&.code;
.fi
.PP
.SH "What Sucrase is not"
.PP
Sucrase is intended to be useful for the most common cases, but it does not aim to have nearly the scope and versatility of Babel\&. Some specific examples:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Sucrase does not check your code for errors\&. Sucrase's contract is that if you give it valid code, it will produce valid JS code\&. If you give it invalid code, it might produce invalid code, it might produce valid code, or it might give an error\&. Always use Sucrase with a linter or typechecker, which is more suited for error-checking\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Sucrase is not pluginizable\&. With the current architecture, transforms need to be explicitly written to cooperate with each other, so each additional transform takes significant extra work\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Sucrase is not good for prototyping language extensions and upcoming language features\&. Its faster architecture makes new transforms more difficult to write and more fragile\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Sucrase will never produce code for old browsers like IE\&. Compiling code down to ES5 is much more complicated than any transformation that Sucrase needs to do\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Sucrase is hesitant to implement upcoming JS features, although some of them make sense to implement for pragmatic reasons\&. Its main focus is on language extensions (JSX, TypeScript, Flow) that will never be supported by JS runtimes\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Like Babel, Sucrase is not a typechecker, and must process each file in isolation\&. For example, TypeScript \fRconst enum\fPs are treated as regular \fRenum\fPs rather than inlining across files\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
You should think carefully before using Sucrase in production\&. Sucrase is mostly beneficial in development, and in many cases, Babel or tsc will be more suitable for production builds\&.
.PP

.PP
See the \fBProject Vision\fP document for more details on the philosophy behind Sucrase\&.
.SH "Motivation"
.PP
As JavaScript implementations mature, it becomes more and more reasonable to disable Babel transforms, especially in development when you know that you're targeting a modern runtime\&. You might hope that you could simplify and speed up the build step by eventually disabling Babel entirely, but this isn't possible if you're using a non-standard language extension like JSX, TypeScript, or Flow\&. Unfortunately, disabling most transforms in Babel doesn't speed it up as much as you might expect\&. To understand, let's take a look at how Babel works:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Tokenize the input source code into a token stream\&.
.IP "2." 4
Parse the token stream into an AST\&.
.IP "3." 4
Walk the AST to compute the scope information for each variable\&.
.IP "4." 4
Apply all transform plugins in a single traversal, resulting in a new AST\&.
.IP "5." 4
Print the resulting AST\&.
.PP

.PP
Only step 4 gets faster when disabling plugins, so there's always a fixed cost to running Babel regardless of how many transforms are enabled\&.

.PP
Sucrase bypasses most of these steps, and works like this:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Tokenize the input source code into a token stream using a trimmed-down fork of the Babel parser\&. This fork does not produce a full AST, but still produces meaningful token metadata specifically designed for the later transforms\&.
.IP "2." 4
Scan through the tokens, computing preliminary information like all imported/exported names\&.
.IP "3." 4
Run the transform by doing a pass through the tokens and performing a number of careful find-and-replace operations, like replacing \fR<Foo\fP with \fRReact\&.createElement(Foo\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
Because Sucrase works on a lower level and uses a custom parser for its use case, it is much faster than Babel\&.
